<main class="lux-article-page"><nav class="lux-breadcrumb" aria-label="Breadcrumb"><a href="<?php echo esc_url(luxureat_static_url('en', '')); ?>">Home</a><span aria-hidden="true">/</span><a href="<?php echo esc_url(luxureat_static_url('en/recipe', '')); ?>">Recipes</a><span aria-hidden="true">/</span><span aria-current="page">Tuscan Panzanella</span></nav><article class="lux-recipe-reader lux-recipe-seo-page"><section class="lux-recipe-hero"><figure><img width="500" height="334" loading="eager" fetchpriority="high" decoding="async" src="<?php echo esc_url(get_template_directory_uri() . '/assets/media/journal/recipe-olive-panzanella.webp'); ?>" alt="Tuscan Panzanella"></figure><div class="lux-recipe-intro"><span>Olive Oil Recipes</span><h1 id="lux-reader-title">Tuscan Panzanella</h1><p>Panzanella is Tuscany’s classic stale-bread salad. Softened bread absorbs tomato, vinegar and extra-virgin olive oil while retaining enough structure for a fresh summer dish.</p><dl class="lux-recipe-facts"><div><dt>Time</dt><dd>About 55 min, including resting</dd></div><div><dt>Difficulty</dt><dd>Easy</dd></div><div><dt>Serves</dt><dd>Serves 4</dd></div></dl></div></section><section class="lux-recipe-body"><aside class="lux-recipe-ingredients"><h2>Ingredients</h2><ul><li>240 g stale country bread</li><li>500 g ripe tomatoes</li><li>180 g cucumber</li><li>80 g red onion</li><li>12 g fresh basil</li><li>40 g extra-virgin olive oil (about 44 ml)</li><li>20 g red-wine vinegar</li><li>About 120 ml cold potable water</li><li>3 g fine salt</li><li>Freshly ground black pepper</li></ul></aside><div class="lux-recipe-method"><h2>Method</h2><ol><li>Cut the bread into 1 cm slices or large pieces and sprinkle evenly with cold water and half the vinegar.</li><li>Rest for 10–15 minutes so the bread softens without becoming mushy.</li><li>Cut the tomatoes, slice the cucumber and thinly slice the onion. If the onion is too sharp, soak in cold water for 10 minutes and drain thoroughly.</li><li>Gently squeeze excess water from the bread and tear it into even pieces.</li><li>Combine bread, tomatoes, cucumber, onion and basil.</li><li>Add the remaining vinegar, oil, salt and pepper and toss gently.</li><li>Refrigerate for about 30 minutes. Toss again before serving and adjust acidity and salt.</li></ol></div></section><section class="lux-recipe-nutrition"><header><h2>Estimated nutrition per serving</h2></header><dl><div><dt>Energy</dt><dd>About 1,160 kJ / 280 kcal</dd></div><div><dt>Fat</dt><dd>About 12 g</dd></div><div><dt>of which saturates</dt><dd>About 2 g</dd></div><div><dt>Carbohydrate</dt><dd>About 37 g</dd></div><div><dt>of which sugars</dt><dd>About 7 g</dd></div><div><dt>Protein</dt><dd>About 7 g</dd></div><div><dt>Fibre</dt><dd>About 4 g</dd></div><div><dt>Sodium</dt><dd>About 530 mg</dd></div></dl><p class="lux-recipe-nutrition-note"><strong>Nutrition note</strong>Estimated per serving; bread type, bread salt and actual seasoning will affect the result.</p></section><section class="lux-recipe-details"><div><dt>Reference region</dt><dd>Tuscany</dd></div><div><dt>Suggested oil</dt><dd>A Tuscan-style extra-virgin olive oil with clear fruit and moderate bitterness and pepperiness.</dd></div><div><dt>Professional tip</dt><dd>The bread should be moist but structured, never a paste. Tomato juice, vinegar and oil continue to soften it, so do not over-wet it initially.</dd></div><div><dt>Food safety</dt><dd>Wash all raw vegetables and herbs under potable running water. Keep refrigerated, eat the same day and do not leave in a warm environment.</dd></div><div><dt>Allergen note</dt><dd>Contains gluten cereals (bread).</dd></div><div><dt>Substitutions</dt><dd>Replace traditional unsalted Tuscan bread with firm stale country bread; white-wine vinegar may replace red-wine vinegar.</dd></div><div><dt>Related products</dt><dd><a class="lux-recipe-product-link" href="<?php echo esc_url(luxureat_static_url('en/product', '?category=olive-oil#product-catalogue')); ?>">A structured LuxurEat (露意膳) extra-virgin olive oil</a></dd></div></section></article></main>
